            <!-- Overview Section -->
            <section id="overview" class="section">
                <h1>Overview</h1>
                
                <h2>Data Source</h2>
                <p>Our data source draws from the CropWizard document corpus, which consists of 400K+ publications, blogs, newsletters, and articles from three categories of sources.</p>
                
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Extension websites from 55+ U.S. land-grant universities</strong> (largest share).</li>
                    <li>Similar documents from other universities in the U.S. and abroad.</li>
                    <li>Open-access research publications from a wide range of agricultural journals.</li>
                </ul>
                
                <p>We only use the PDFs from the CropWizard corpus.</p>

                <h2>Document Filtering</h2>
                <p>We process original CropWizard documents to select relevant, high-quality content:</p>

                <h3>Hard Filtering</h3>
                <p>Rule-based quality screening removes low-content materials, poorly scanned documents, and OCR noise artifacts.</p>

                <h3>Semantic Filtering</h3>
                <p>Documents are segmented using a hierarchical chunking method, embedded with a large-scale sentence transformer model (Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B), and compared against seed queries from nine agricultural categories:</p>

                <ul>
                    <li>Crop Management</li>
                    <li>Pests & Pest Management</li>
                    <li>Weeds & Weed Management</li>
                    <li>Nutrition</li>
                    <li>Water</li>
                    <li>Soil Health</li>
                    <li>Crop Marketing</li>
                    <li>Farm Finance</li>
                    <li>Climate & Weather Risk</li>
                </ul>

                <p>To ensure these seed queries comprehensively cover agricultural knowledge, we automatically generate them from the CropWizard corpus using an LLM (gpt-4.1). Retrieved text chunks are transformed into concise, actionable queries, then clustered and manually reviewed to produce a high-quality, non-redundant query set that guides semantic filtering.</p>

                <div class="method-callout">
                    <h3>Method at a Glance</h3>
                    <p><strong>Ingest PDFs → Hard Filter → Embed & Retrieve → Cluster & Curate</strong></p>
                </div>
            <div class="hero-resource-buttons">
